Day 269/300: Remove Nth node from end of List

Problem Statement #19

Problem Statement EXplantion:

Given the head of a linked list, remove the nth node from the end of the list and return its head.

Example 1:

Input: head = [1,2,3,4,5], n = 2
Output: [1,2,3,5]
Example 2:
Input: head = [1], n = 1
Output: []

Solution Approach:

To solve this in a single pass, we use two pointers (fast and slow) and a dummy node.

Initialize: Create a dummy node that points to the head. 
This handles edge cases, such as removing the head of the list.
Create a Gap: Advance the fast pointer n+1 steps forward. 
This creates a gap of n nodes between the slow and fast pointers.
Traverse: Move both pointers forward one step at a time until the fast pointer reaches null.
Delete: Because of the gap, the slow pointer will now be pointing to the node immediately before the one we want to remove. 
We skip the target node by setting slow.next = slow.next.next.
Return: Return dummy.next.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        
        ListNode fast = dummy;
        ListNode slow = dummy;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            fast = fast.next;
        }

        while (fast != null) {
            fast = fast.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }

        slow.next = slow.next.next;

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(L)
Space Complexity: O(1)



